.TH DHCP-LEASE-LIST 8 "2026-08-24" "dhcp-oru-toolkit 2.1.2" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
dhcp-lease-list \- display ISC or Kea DHCP IPv4 and IPv6 leases
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dhcp-lease-list
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dhcp-lease-list
reads ISC DHCP (\fIdhcpd.leases\fR / \fIdhcpd6.leases\fR) or Kea DHCP
(\fImemfile\fR CSV) lease databases and prints a unified, color-coded view of
IPv4 and IPv6 leases. It extracts the client MAC address from DHCPv6 DUIDs
(DUID-LLT, DUID-EN, DUID-LL) and, for Kea DHCPv4, from the option 61
client-identifier, so units are identifiable by MAC on either server.
.PP
By default the command detects which DHCP server is installed; see
.B SERVER SELECTION
below.
.PP
With
.B \-\-conflicts
it additionally reports lease conflicts: a single MAC holding multiple active
IP addresses, or a single IP address held by multiple active MACs \(em the
signature of the O-RU shared-xid IP-theft defect.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-server {auto,isc,kea,both}
DHCP server type (default: auto). \fBauto\fR detects what is installed,
\fBboth\fR reports ISC and Kea in separate sections, and \fBisc\fR or
\fBkea\fR force a single server.
.TP
.B \-\-v4\-lease \fIFILE\fR
Path to the IPv4 lease file (overrides the path for the selected server).
.TP
.B \-\-v6\-lease \fIFILE\fR
Path to the IPv6 lease file (overrides the path for the selected server).
.TP
.B \-\-kea\-config\-dir \fIDIR\fR
Directory holding \fIkea-dhcp4.conf\fR and \fIkea-dhcp6.conf\fR
(default: \fI/etc/kea\fR, then \fI/usr/local/etc/kea\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-v4\-only
Show IPv4 leases only.
.TP
.B \-\-v6\-only
Show IPv6 leases only.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Show all leases, including expired and free.
.TP
.B \-\-state {active,free,expired,declined,released}
Filter leases by binding state.
.TP
.B \-\-conflicts
After listing, scan the parsed leases for conflicts (one MAC holding multiple
active IPs, or one IP held by multiple active MACs) and print them. Exits
non-zero (2) if any HIGH-severity conflict is found.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the program version and exit.
.SH SERVER SELECTION
With the default
.BR \-\-server " " auto :
.IP \(bu 3
If \fB\-\-v4\-lease\fR or \fB\-\-v6\-lease\fR names a file, its format is
sniffed \(em a Kea CSV header or ISC \fIlease\fR/\fIia-na\fR blocks \(em and
the matching parser is used. Naming a lease file therefore does not require
\fB\-\-server\fR as well.
.IP \(bu 3
Otherwise ISC is reported when its lease files exist, and Kea when a Kea
config or lease file exists. A host running both gets a section for each.
.IP \(bu 3
If neither is found, ISC is assumed, as in earlier releases.
.SH LEASE FILE LOCATIONS
.TP
.B ISC
/var/lib/dhcp/dhcpd.leases and /var/lib/dhcp/dhcpd6.leases.
.TP
.B Kea
Read from the \fBname\fR entry of the \fBlease-database\fR block
in \fIkea-dhcp4.conf\fR / \fIkea-dhcp6.conf\fR, falling back to Kea's own
defaults, /var/lib/kea/kea-leases4.csv and /var/lib/kea/kea-leases6.csv, when
no config can be read. The Kea config dialect (\fB//\fR, \fB#\fR and
\fB/* */\fR comments and \fB<?include?>\fR directives) is handled.
.PP
Kea's Lease File Cleanup (LFC) does not rewrite the lease file in place: it
moves the current file aside and consolidates the old generations through
\fI<name>.1\fR, \fI<name>.2\fR and \fI<name>.completed\fR. Those generations
are read alongside the primary file, in the same order Kea reloads them, so
leases are still listed while a cleanup is in flight \(em or indefinitely, if
LFC was interrupted by a crash. A
.B [NOTE]
line names each generation that was included.
.PP
When Kea is configured with a
.B mysql
or
.B postgresql
lease backend, or a
.B memfile
with \fBpersist\fR set to \fBfalse\fR,
there is no lease file to read. This is reported explicitly; use
.BR kea-shell (8)
with the
.B lease4-get-all
/
.B lease6-get-all
commands, or query the database directly.
.SH FILE PERMISSIONS
Lease databases are not world-readable. A stock Ubuntu Kea install ships
.I /var/lib/kea
and the lease files inside it owned by the
.B _kea
user, and
.I /etc/kea
likewise, so an unprivileged run reads nothing. ISC's
.I /var/lib/dhcp/dhcpd.leases
is conventionally world-readable, so this chiefly affects Kea.
.PP
Either run under
.BR sudo (8),
or grant the account read access.
.PP
Do
.B not
chmod the Kea lease files. LFC does not edit them in place: it writes a new
file and renames it over the old one, so the replacement carries Kea's own
default permissions and a manual mode change is lost at the next cleanup.
Grant access durably instead, by adding the account to the
.B _kea
group \(em the lease files are already group-readable, and so are the files
LFC creates:
.PP
.RS 4
.B usermod -aG _kea \fIuser\fR
.RE
.PP
Group membership takes effect at the account's next login. If
.I /var/lib/kea
itself is not group-searchable, grant that once with
.BR "chmod g+rx /var/lib/kea" :
directory permissions, unlike the files inside, are not touched by LFC.
.PP
For per-user access without group membership, use an ACL with a default entry
so that lease files created later inherit it:
.PP
.RS 4
.B setfacl -m u:\fIuser\fB:rx /var/lib/kea
.br
.B setfacl -d -m u:\fIuser\fB:r /var/lib/kea
.RE
.PP
A lease file that exists but cannot be read is reported as a permission error
naming the directory, not as a missing file.
.SH LEASE STATE
Kea's memfile is a journal: each lease update appends a row, the last row for
an address is authoritative, and a row with a
.B valid_lifetime
of 0 marks the lease deleted. Released, declined and reclaimed addresses are
therefore reported as such rather than as still held. Kea state codes map as
0=active, 1=declined, 2=expired, 3=released.
.PP
Lease timestamps are UTC on both servers, and expiry is evaluated against UTC.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
List active leases from whichever server is installed:
.B dhcp-lease-list
.TP
Show every lease including expired/free:
.B dhcp-lease-list \-\-all
.TP
Read Kea lease databases explicitly:
.B dhcp-lease-list \-\-server kea
.TP
Report both DHCP servers on a host that runs each:
.B dhcp-lease-list \-\-server both
.TP
Inspect a specific lease file (format detected automatically):
.B dhcp-lease-list \-\-v4\-only \-\-v4\-lease /var/lib/kea/kea-leases4.csv
.TP
Use a Kea config kept outside /etc/kea:
.B dhcp-lease-list \-\-kea\-config\-dir /opt/kea/etc
.TP
Flag IP-theft-style conflicts:
.B dhcp-lease-list \-\-conflicts
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success, and 2 when
.B \-\-conflicts
finds a HIGH-severity conflict.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dhcp-forensics (8),
.BR dhcpd (8),
.BR kea-dhcp4 (8),
.BR kea-dhcp6 (8),
.BR kea-lfc (8),
.BR kea-shell (8)
